------------------------------------------------------------
Rule rename experiment
------------------------------------------------------------

Question:
    Can an NSG rule be renamed directly?

Conclusion:
    No direct rename operation was used/available.

Solution:
    Delete the old rule and create a new rule.

Deleted:
    Allow-Subnet2-8080

Created:
    Allow-Subnet2-443

Configuration:

Priority:
    100

Direction:
    Inbound

Access:
    Allow

Protocol:
    TCP

Source:
    10.0.1.0/24

Destination:
    10.0.2.0/24

Destination port:
    443


------------------------------------------------------------
NSG 1 deny rule
------------------------------------------------------------

Created:

    Deny-Subnet1-to-Subnet2-443

Priority:
    100

Direction:
    Outbound

Access:
    Deny

Protocol:
    TCP

Source:
    10.0.1.0/24

Destination:
    10.0.2.0/24

Destination port:
    443

Purpose:
    Demonstrate NSG rule priority and rule evaluation.


------------------------------------------------------------
NSG 1 allow rule
------------------------------------------------------------

Initial attempt contained a typo:

    --source-address-prefoxes

Result:
    Azure CLI rejected the argument.

Corrected:

    --source-address-prefixes

Created:

    Allow-Subnet1-to-Subnet2-443

Initial priority:
    200

Direction:
    Outbound

Access:
    Allow

Protocol:
    TCP

Source:
    10.0.1.0/24

Destination:
    10.0.2.0/24

Destination port:
    443


------------------------------------------------------------
Priority experiment
------------------------------------------------------------

Initial state:

    Deny-Subnet1-to-Subnet2-443    Priority 100
    Allow-Subnet1-to-Subnet2-443   Priority 200

Attempted to change priority to:
    50

Result:
    Failed.

Error:
    SecurityRuleInvalidPriority

Allowed range:
    100-4096

Learning:
    NSG custom rule priorities must be between 100 and 4096.


------------------------------------------------------------
Priority conflict experiment
------------------------------------------------------------

Attempted to change:

    Deny-Subnet1-to-Subnet2-443

from priority:
    100

to:
    200

Result:
    Failed.

Reason:
    Priority 200 was already occupied by another outbound rule.

Error:
    SecurityRuleConflict

Learning:
    NSG rules cannot have the same priority when they have the
    same direction.


------------------------------------------------------------
Successfully reordered rules
------------------------------------------------------------

Changed:

    Deny-Subnet1-to-Subnet2-443
        100 -> 300

Then changed:

    Allow-Subnet1-to-Subnet2-443
        200 -> 100

Final state:

    Allow-Subnet1-to-Subnet2-443
        Priority: 100
        Direction: Outbound
        Access: Allow

    Deny-Subnet1-to-Subnet2-443
        Priority: 300
        Direction: Outbound
        Access: Deny

Important learning:

    NSG rules are evaluated in priority order.

    Lower numerical priority is evaluated before higher numerical
    priority.

Therefore:

    Priority 100 Allow
        is evaluated before
    Priority 300 Deny

The allow rule wins for traffic matching that rule.


