    VM2 NIC:
        No NIC-level NSG


------------------------------------------------------------
Inbound deny rule on NSG1
------------------------------------------------------------

Created:

    Deny-Subnet2-to-Subnet1-443

Priority:
    100

Direction:
    Inbound

Access:
    Deny

Protocol:
    TCP

Source:
    10.0.2.0/24

Destination:
    10.0.1.0/24

Destination port:
    443

Purpose:
    Block TCP/443 traffic originating from subnet 2
    and entering subnet 1.


------------------------------------------------------------
NSG1 final rule state
------------------------------------------------------------

    Allow-Subnet1-to-Subnet2-443
        Priority: 100
        Direction: Outbound
        Access: Allow
        Source: 10.0.1.0/24
        Destination: 10.0.2.0/24
        Port: 443

    Deny-Subnet2-to-Subnet1-443
        Priority: 100
        Direction: Inbound
        Access: Deny
        Source: 10.0.2.0/24
        Destination: 10.0.1.0/24
        Port: 443

Important:
    Both rules use priority 100, but they are in different
    directions. This is valid because priority uniqueness is
    enforced within the relevant direction.


------------------------------------------------------------
Directional connectivity test — blocked direction
------------------------------------------------------------

From VM2:

    timeout 5 nc -vz 10.0.1.4 443

Result:

    No successful connection message.

Interpretation:

    VM2 -> VM1 TCP/443 was blocked.

The inbound deny rule on VM1's NSG matched the traffic.


------------------------------------------------------------
Directional connectivity test — allowed direction
------------------------------------------------------------

From VM1:

    timeout 5 nc -vz 10.0.2.4 443

Result:

    Connection to 10.0.2.4 443 port [tcp/https] succeeded!

Interpretation:

    VM1 -> VM2 TCP/443 remained allowed.

This demonstrates that NSG rules can enforce directional
communication between subnets.


